# Engineering Rules (Java)

## Build System Is Authoritative
- The repository's own build is the only way to compile and test. Use Maven or
  Gradle exactly as this repository configures it.
- Do not hand-roll test runners, invoke `javac` directly, or call the JUnit
  launcher yourself to work around the build.
- Do not change dependency versions, add dependencies, or edit CI
  configuration. If a change seems to require one, stop and ask the operator.
- Do not change formatter, Checkstyle, PMD, or SpotBugs configuration. Follow
  what the repository already enforces.

## Test Loop Economy
Java builds are slow and every handoff costs a full audit pass. Scope your runs.

- During the red-green loop, run the narrowest thing that proves the behavior:
  - Maven: `mvn -q -pl <module> -am -Dtest=<TestClass> test`
  - Gradle: `./gradlew :<module>:test --tests '<TestClass>'`
- Run the project's full verification exactly once, immediately before you
  queue a handoff:
  - Maven: `mvn -q verify`
  - Gradle: `./gradlew build`
- Do not run the full suite after every cycle.
- If the full build is red for a reason your task did not cause, say so in the
  audit rather than fixing unrelated modules.

## Testing
- JUnit 5 with AssertJ assertions, Mockito for collaborators, unless this
  repository already standardizes on something else. Match the existing style.
- Do not mock types you do not own. Wrap third-party and framework types in a
  narrow adapter interface and mock the adapter.
- For each behavior slice, write failing unit tests that express the requested
  observable behavior first, then the minimum production code that passes them.
- A test must fail for a plausible wrong implementation. A test that passes
  against an empty method body is not a test.
- Mirror the source layout: `src/test/java` beside `src/main/java`.

## Coverage, Mutation, Duplication
Run these one at a time. Never concurrently, never repository-wide.

- Coverage: JaCoCo through the build (`mvn verify` with the jacoco plugin, or
  `./gradlew jacocoTestReport`). Look at branches in code this task touched.
- Mutation: PIT, always scoped to the touched classes:
  `mvn -q org.pitest:pitest-maven:mutationCoverage -DtargetClasses=<pkg>.<Class> -DtargetTests=<pkg>.<Class>Test -DwithHistory`
  A surviving mutant is a missing test. Kill it by adding the test, never by
  deleting the code path the mutant altered.
- Duplication: PMD CPD (`mvn pmd:cpd`, or the repository's configured task).
  Report duplication this change introduced or worsened.

## Architecture
- Express architectural rules as ArchUnit tests, not prose: dependency
  direction, package boundaries, layer isolation, forbidden imports.
- A structural finding worth stating is worth enforcing. If it can become an
  ArchUnit rule, make it one so it survives this task.
- Keep high-level policy independent of IO, UI, framework, filesystem,
  database, network, and device details. Adapters depend inward.

## Design And Testability
- Work in small, reviewable increments.
- Prefer the simplest design that supports current behavior and leaves clear
  options for the next step.
- Separate pure, testable logic from environment boundaries. Keep the
  untestable boundary thin and behind an interface.
- Only testable code participates in coverage, mutation, and duplication
  analysis.

## Optional CRAP Metric
- CRAP combines cyclomatic complexity with coverage:
  `CRAP = CC^2 * (1 - coverage)^3 + CC`. There is no mainstream Java tool for
  it. `github.com/unclebob/crap4java` implements it on top of JaCoCo.
- Use it only if the operator has already installed it. Do not install it
  yourself, and do not substitute a homegrown proxy. Without it, report
  complexity concerns in prose against the touched methods.

## Guardrails
- Do not commit unrelated changes or generated artifacts.
- Do not reformat files this task did not touch.
- Do not weaken, delete, `@Disabled`, or `@Ignore` a test to reach green. A
  failing test is a finding, not an obstacle.
- Do not add a new third-party dependency. Ask the operator instead.
- Before relying on an unfamiliar command, read this repository's build files
  and developer documentation.
